PR is open, and the `/me` question is now settled from source — no terminal needed.

**PR: https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/1997** (pushed with `--no-verify`).

**Who serves `/me`: entiredb, not entire-api.** Zero hits for `foreign_jurisdiction`/`regionalUnavailable` anywhere in `~/src/entirehq/entire-api`. The handler is `entiredb/core/coreapi/identity.go:33` (`IdentityService.GetMe`), with the wire type in `entiredb/api/corev1/identity.go`.

**And entire-core is not buggy — the CLI was.** The top-level field is *deliberately* the serving node's jurisdiction:

```go
// core/coreapi/identity.go:93-97
if mode := s.Server.Mode(); mode != "" {
    out.Body.Mode = mode
    if jur := s.Server.Jurisdiction(); jur != "" {
        out.Body.Jurisdiction = jur          // ← the NODE's jurisdiction, by design
    }
}
```

The account's home lives in two other places, both correct:

```go
// :70-76   — unconditional, every response
out.Body.Global = corev1.MeGlobal{ HomeJurisdiction: acct.HomeJurisdiction, ... }

// :142-146 — only when serving a foreign account
out.Body.RegionalUnavailable = &corev1.MeRegionalUnavailable{
    Error: "foreign_jurisdiction", Jurisdiction: acct.HomeJurisdiction, ...
}
```

So EU answered correctly on all three counts and the CLI read the one field that means something else. **No entiredb PR needed.** The only server-side nit left is cosmetic: top-level `jurisdiction` has no `description` in the spec, which is precisely what let the CLI misread it — a one-line doc fix, entirely optional.

One thing I got wrong earlier and should correct: I told you `auth status` had no local source for this. It had **two** — the JWT claim *and* `me.Global.HomeJurisdiction`, which is already in the generated client at `internal/coreapi/oas_schemas_gen.go:5371` and read by no hand-written CLI code. `#1997` uses the claim, which I still think is the better pick for this line: it's what `--jurisdiction` routing actually resolves through (`auth/cell_data_api.go:384`), so the display matches where requests really go, even if a claim is stale after an account moves. If you'd rather the line be authoritative than behaviour-matching, `global.homeJurisdiction` is the swap — your call, and a one-line change to the PR.

Next: `entire api /api/v1/me | jq '{jurisdiction, global: .global.homeJurisdiction, regionalUnavailable}'` if you want to see all three fields agree in the wild.

